"We have the system of equations\n\n\\begin{align*}\np+q&=pq\\\\\np-q-q&=7.\n\\end{align*}\n\nFrom the second equation, we have $p-q=7$. Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$p+q=7p\\Rightarrow p=\\frac{q}{7}.$$\n\nNow, we need to find $\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{p^2}+\\frac{1}{q^2}}$. Substituting $p=\\frac{q}{7}$, we get\n\n$$\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{q}{7}\\right)^2}+\\frac{1}{q^2}}=\\frac{1}{\\frac{49}{q^2}+\\frac{1}{q^2}}=\\frac{1}{\\frac{50}{q^2}}=\\frac{q^2}{50}.$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{p^2}+\\frac{1}{q^2}}=\\frac{q^2}{50}$.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $\\boxed{50}$."
