"To determine the rock-climbing shoe size for Adina, we need to use the given information about Lynn's shoe size and rock-climbing shoe size. We know that Lynn's normal shoe size is 9 and her rock-climbing shoe size is 42. Since shoe size is directly proportional to rock-climbing shoe size, we can set up a proportion to find Adina's rock-climbing shoe size.\n\nLet \\( x \\) be Adina's rock-climbing shoe size. The proportion can be written as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{9}{42} = \\frac{6}{x}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( x \\), we cross-multiply:\n\n\\[\n9x = 42 \\times 6\n\\]\n\n\\[\n9x = 252\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{252}{9}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = 28\n\\]\n\nTherefore, Adina should rent \\(\\boxed{28}\\) size rock-climbing shoes."
"To find the value of \\( c \\) such that the polynomial \\( f(x) + cg(x) \\) has degree 2, we need to analyze the degrees of the polynomials \\( f(x) \\) and \\( g(x) \\).\n\nThe polynomial \\( f(x) = 4x^3 + 3x^2 + 2x + 1 \\) has a degree of 3. The polynomial \\( g(x) = 3 - 4x + 5x^2 - 6x^3 \\) has a degree of 3.\n\nFor \\( f(x) + cg(x) \\) to have a degree of 2, the highest term of \\( f(x) \\) must be canceled by the highest term of \\( g(x) \\). This means that the coefficient of \\( x^3 \\) in \\( g(x) \\) must be equal to the coefficient of \\( x^3 \\) in \\( f(x) \\).\n\nThus, we need:\n\\[ -6c = 4 \\]\n\nSolving for \\( c \\):\n\\[ c = -\\frac{2}{3} \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( c \\) is \\(\\boxed{-\\frac{2}{3}}\\)."
