"To solve the equation \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{6y+2}}{\\sqrt{2y}}=\\frac{5}{2}\\), we start by squaring both sides to eliminate the square roots:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{6y+2}}{\\sqrt{2y}}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{6y+2}{2y} = \\frac{25}{4}\n\\]\n\nNext, we cross-multiply to solve for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n4(6y + 2) = 25(2y)\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n24y + 8 = 50y\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\(24y\\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n8 = 26y\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 26:\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{8}{26} = \\frac{4}{13}\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution to the equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{4}{13}}\n\\]"
"The population of bacteria doubles every four hours. Therefore, we need to determine how many times the population doubles in the twelve-hour period from noon to midnight.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of four-hour intervals in the twelve hours:\n\\[\n\\frac{12 \\text{ hours}}{4 \\text{ hours/interval}} = 3 \\text{ intervals}\n\\]\n\nSince the population doubles every four hours, the population will double three times in the twelve-hour period.\n\nNext, we calculate the population after each doubling:\n\\[\nFirst doubling: \\ 600 \\times 2 = 1200\n\\]\n\\[\nSecond doubling: \\ 1200 \\times 2 = 2400\n\\]\n\\[\nThird doubling: \\ 2400 \\times 2 = 4800\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the population of bacteria at midnight will be $\\boxed{4800}$."
