"To expand the expression \\((q-5)(3r+6)\\), we use the distributive property (also known as the FOIL method for binomials):\n\n\\begin{align*}\n(q-5)(3r+6) &= q \\cdot 3r + q \\cdot 6 - 5 \\cdot 3r - 5 \\cdot 6 \\\\\n&= 3qr + 6q - 15r - 30 \\\\\n&= 3qr + 9q - 15r - 30.\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the expanded form of \\((q-5)(3r+6)\\) is \\(\\boxed{3qr + 9q - 15r - 30}\\)."
"We can combine the fractions on the left-hand side by finding a common denominator:\n\n$$\\frac{1}{x-1} + \\frac{2x}{x - 1} = \\frac{1 + 2x}{x-1} = \\frac{2x + 1}{x-1}.$$\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n$$\\frac{2x + 1}{x-1} = 5.$$\n\nWe can clear the fraction by multiplying both sides by $x-1$:\n\n$$2x + 1 = 5(x - 1).$$\n\nExpanding and simplifying, we get:\n\n$$2x + 1 = 5x - 5.$$\n\nSubtracting $2x$ from both sides, we get:\n\n$$1 = 3x - 5.$$\n\nAdding 5 to both sides, we get:\n\n$$6 = 3x.$$\n\nDividing both sides by 3, we get:\n\n$$x = 2.$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x = \\boxed{2}$.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $2$. I hope it is correct."
