"We need to solve the inequality $x^2 + 4x + 4 < 20$ for positive integer values of $x$.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the inequality:\n\\[x^2 + 4x + 4 < 20\\]\n\\[x^2 + 4x - 16 < 0\\]\n\nNext, we factor the quadratic expression:\n\\[(x + 6)(x - 2) < 0\\]\n\nThis inequality holds when:\n\\[-6 < x < 2\\]\n\nSince we are looking for positive integer values of $x$, we consider the interval:\n\\[0 < x \\leq 2\\]\n\nThe positive integer values of $x$ that satisfy this inequality are:\n\\[x = 1, 2\\]\n\nTherefore, there are $\\boxed{2}$ positive integer values of $x$ for which the sum $x^2 + 4x + 4 < 20$."
