"To determine the probability that the product of the numbers picked by Krishanu and Shaunak more than 10, we need to consider the conditions under which this product is true.\n\nThe product of two numbers is more than 10 if at least one of the numbers is greater than 1. Therefore, we need to find the probability that at least one of the numbers is greater than 1.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the probability that both numbers are less than or equal to 1. There are 10 possible outcomes for each number, and only one of these outcomes is less than or equal to 1. Therefore, the probability that both numbers are less than or equal to 1 is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{10} \\times \\frac{1}{10} = \\frac{1}{100}\n\\]\n\nThe probability that at least one of the numbers is greater than 1 is the complement of this probability:\n\n\\[\n1 - \\frac{1}{100} = \\frac{99}{100}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{99}{100}}\\)."
