"To find the product \\(\\frac{25}{3} \\cdot \\frac{27}{300}\\), we can multiply the numerators together and the denominators together:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{25}{3} \\cdot \\frac{27}{300} = \\frac{25 \\cdot 27}{3 \\cdot 300} = \\frac{675}{900}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the fraction by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 675 and 900. The GCD of 675 and 900 is 75. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by their GCD:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{675 \\div 75}{900 \\div 75} = \\frac{9}{12}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{9}{12}}\\)."
"First, convert the mixed number to a fraction:\n\\[ 4\\dfrac{5}{8} = \\frac{37}{8} \\]\n\nNow, rewrite the expression:\n\\[ \\left(\\frac{37}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{8}{37}\\right)^{55} \\]\n\nUsing the properties of exponents, we can combine the fractions:\n\\[ \\left(\\frac{37}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\frac{8}{37}\\right)^{55} = \\left(\\frac{37}{8} \\cdot \\frac{8}{37}\\right)^{55} \\]\n\nSimplify the fraction inside the parentheses:\n\\[ \\frac{37}{8} \\cdot \\frac{8}{37} = 1 \\]\n\nSo, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[ 1^{55} = 1 \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1} \\]"
"To solve the expression \\(\\frac{7+21}{14+42}\\), we first perform the addition in the numerator and the denominator.\n\nThe numerator is:\n\\[ 7 + 21 = 28 \\]\n\nThe denominator is:\n\\[ 14 + 42 = 56 \\]\n\nSo, the expression becomes:\n\\[ \\frac{28}{56} \\]\n\nNext, we simplify the fraction by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 28 and 56. The GCD of 28 and 56 is 4.\n\nWe divide both the numerator and the denominator by their GCD:\n\\[ \\frac{28 \\div 4}{56 \\div 4} = \\frac{7}{14} \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the expression is:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{7}{14}} \\]"
