"The area of the original square is $49$ square inches, so the side length of the square is $\\sqrt{49} = 7$ inches.\n\nWhen the square is folded, the two congruent triangles formed are isosceles right triangles. The legs of each triangle are equal to the side length of the square, which is $7$ inches.\n\nTo find the hypotenuse of each triangle, we use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\n\\text{Hypotenuse} = \\sqrt{7^2 + 7^2} = \\sqrt{49 + 49} = \\sqrt{98} = 7\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThe perimeter of one triangle is the sum of the lengths of its sides:\n\\[\n\\text{Perimeter} = 7 + 7 + 7\\sqrt{2} = 14 + 7\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of one of the triangles is $\\boxed{14 + 7\\sqrt{2}}$ inches."
